Here's an example of how you might configure comments in a Java-based project:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CommentsConfiguration {
    private Map<String, List<String>> commentMap;

    public void setComment(String key, List<String> value) {
        if (commentMap == null) {
            commentMap = new HashMap<>();
        }
        commentMap.put(key, value);
    }

    public List<String> getComments() {
        return commentMap != null ? commentMap.values().iterator().next() : Collections.emptyList();
    }

    public Map<String, @UnmodifiableView List<String>> getAllComments() {
        if (commentMap == null) {
            return Collections.emptyMap();
        }
        return Collections.unmodifiableMap(new HashMap<>(commentMap));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CommentsConfiguration comments = new CommentsConfiguration();

        // Set some comments
        comments.setComment("key1", Arrays.asList("This is a comment", "Another one"));
        comments.setComment("key2", Collections.singletonList("And another one"));

        // Get all comments
        System.out.println(comments.getAllComments());

        // Get individual comments
        System.out.println(comments.getComments());
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `CommentsConfiguration` class that holds a map of comments. We can set comments using the `setComment` method and get them using the `getComments` method. The `getAllComments` method returns an unmodifiable view of all comments.

Please note that this is a very basic example, and you might need to adapt it to your specific requirements.
